{"name":"Golden Crested Wren","hex":"#ccddbb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"crested","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a crest, or ornamental tuft (on an animal) or plume (on a helmet).","Having a heraldic crest, badge, shield-like emblem, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of crest"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crested"},{"term":"wren","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Troglodytes troglodytes (Eurasian wren, type species and sole Old World species of the family Troglodytidae).","Any member of a mainly New World passerine bird family Troglodytidae; a true wren."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wren"}],"entries":[{"title":"Goldcrest","blocks":[{"text":"The goldcrest is a very small passerine bird in the kinglet family. Its colourful golden crest feathers, as well as being called the \"king of the birds\" in European folklore, gives rise to its English and scientific names. The scientific name, R. regulus, means 'petty king' or prince. Several subspecies are recognised across the very large distribution range that includes much of the Palearctic and the islands of Macaronesia and Iceland. Birds from the north and east of its breeding range migrate to winter further south."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Goldcrest_1.jpg/330px-Goldcrest_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldcrest","wikidata":"Q26657"},{"title":"Wren","blocks":[{"text":"Wrens are a family, Troglodytidae, of small brown passerine birds. The family includes 96 species and is divided into 19 genera. All species are restricted to the New World except for the Eurasian wren that is widely distributed in the Old World. In Anglophone regions, the Eurasian wren is commonly known simply as the \"wren\", as it is the originator of the name. The name wren has been applied to other, unrelated birds, particularly the New Zealand wrens (Acanthisittidae) and the Australian wrens (Maluridae)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Troglodytidae_diversity.jpg/330px-Troglodytidae_diversity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wren","wikidata":"Q208304"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Goldcrest_Vinayak_Uttarakhand_India_05.02.2015.jpg/500px-Goldcrest_Vinayak_Uttarakhand_India_05.02.2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Goldcrest_Vinayak_Uttarakhand_India_05.02.2015.jpg/500px-Goldcrest_Vinayak_Uttarakhand_India_05.02.2015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Subspecies R. r. himalayensis at Vinayak village in Uttarakhand, India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoldcrest_Vinayak_Uttarakhand_India_05.02.2015.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Regulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg/500px-Regulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Regulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg/1280px-Regulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Regulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg/500px-Regulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The nominate subspecies, R. r. regulus, in Belgium. The goldcrest has a bright crest and a relatively plain face. The orange tinge of the hindcrown indicates that this is a male.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARegulus_regulus_-Auderghem%2C_Brussels_-head-8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Juvenile_goldcrest.jpg/500px-Juvenile_goldcrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Juvenile_goldcrest.jpg/1280px-Juvenile_goldcrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Juvenile_goldcrest.jpg/500px-Juvenile_goldcrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A juvenile goldcrest in Poland. The head does not yet show the coloured crown and the beak is brighter than in adults.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuvenile_goldcrest.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Regulus_regulus_-Vendee%2C_France-8.jpg/500px-Regulus_regulus_-Vendee%2C_France-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Regulus_regulus_-Vendee%2C_France-8.jpg/500px-Regulus_regulus_-Vendee%2C_France-8.